used for factoring. In fact, this technique reduces the number\nL749@P14: of quantum gates needed for the (approximate) Fourier transform considerably, as it\nL750@P14: leaves out most of the gates Sj,k.\nL751@P14: 5 Prime factorization\nL752@P14: It has been known since before Euclid that every integer n is uniquely decomposable\nL753@P14: into a product of primes. Mathematicians have been interested in the question of how\nL754@P14: to factor a number into this product of primes for nearly as long. It was only in the\nL755@P14: 1970’s, however, that researchers applied the paradigms of theoretical computer science\nL756@P14: to number theory, and looked at the asymptotic running times of factoring algorithms\nL757@P14: [Adleman 1994]. This has resulted in a great improvement in the efficiency of factoring\nL758@P14: algorithms. The best factoring algorithm asymptotically is currently the number field\nL759@P14: sieve [Lenstra et al. 1990, Lenstra and Lenstra 1993], which in order to factor an inte\u0002ger n takes asymptotic running time exp(c(log n)\nL760@P14: 1/3\nL761@P14: (log log n)\nL762@P14: 2/3\nL763@P14: ) for some constant c.\nL764@P14: Since the input, n, is only log n bits in length, this algorithm is an exponential-time\nL765@P14: algorithm. Our quantum factoring algorithm takes asymptotically O((log n)\nL766@P14: 2\nL767@P14: (log log n)\nL768@P14: (log log log n)) steps on a quantum computer, along with a polynomial (in log n) amount\nL769@P14: of post-processing time on a classical computer that is used to convert the output of\nL770@P14: the quantum computer to factors of n. While this post-processing could in principle be\nL771@P14: done on a quantum computer, there is no reason not to use a classical computer if they\nL772@P14: are more efficient in practice.\nL773@P14: Instead of giving a quantum computer algorithm for factoring n directly, we give a\nL774@P14: quantum computer algorithm for finding the order of an element x in the multiplicative\nL775@P14: group (mod n); that is, the least integer r such that x\nL776@P14: r ≡ 1 (mod n). It is known that\nL777@P14: using randomization, factorization can be reduced to finding the order of an element\nL778@P14: [Miller 1976]; we now briefly give this reduction.\nL779@P14: To find a factor of an odd number n, given a method for computing the order r\nL780@P14: of x, choose a random x (mod n), find its order r, and compute gcd(x\nL781@P14: r/2 − 1, n). Here,\nL782@P14: gcd(a, b) is the greatest common divisor of a and b, i.e., the largest integer that divides\nL783@P14: both a and b. The Euclidean algorithm [Knuth 1981] can be used to compute gcd(a, b)\nL784@P14: in polynomial time. Since (x\nL785@P14-15: r/2 −1)(xr/2 + 1) = xr −1 ≡ 0 (mod n), the gcd(xr/2 −1, n)16 P. W. SHOR\nL786@P15: fails to be a non-trivial divisor of n only if r is odd or if x\nL787@P15: r/2 ≡ −1 (mod n). Using this\nL788@P15: criterion, it can be shown that this procedure, when applied to a random x (mod n),\nL789@P15: yields a factor of n with probability at least 1−1/2\nL790@P15: k−1\nL791@P15: , where k is the number of distinct\nL792@P15: odd prime factors of n. A brief sketch of the proof of this result follows. Suppose that\nL793@P15: n =\nL794@P15: Qk\nL795@P15: i=1 p\nL796@P15: ai\nL797@P15: i\nL798@P15: . Let ri be the order of x (mod p\nL799@P15: ai\nL800@P15: i\nL801@P15: ). Then r is the least common multiple\nL802@P15: of all the ri. Consider the largest power of 2 dividing each ri. The algorithm only fails\nL803@P15: if all of these powers of 2 agree: if they are all 1, then r is odd and r/2 does not exist; if\nL804@P15: they are all equal and larger than 1, then x\nL805@P15: r/2 ≡ −1 (mod n) since xr/2 ≡ −1 (mod p\nL806@P15: αi\nL807@P15: i\nL808@P15: )\nL809@P15: for every i. By the Chinese remainder theorem [Knuth 1981, Hardy and Wright 1979,\nL810@P15: Theorem 121], choosing an x (mod n) at random is the same as choosing for each i a\nL811@P15: number xi (mod p\nL812@P15: ai\nL813@P15: i\nL814@P15: ) at random, where p\nL815@P15: ai\nL816@P15: i\nL817@P15: is the ith prime power factor of n. The\nL818@P15: multiplicative group (mod p\nL819@P15: α) for any odd prime power pα is cyclic [Knuth 1981], so for\nL820@P15: any odd prime power p\nL821@P15: ai\nL822@P15: i\nL823@P15: , the probability is at most 1/2 of choosing an xi having any\nL824@P15: particular power of two as the largest divisor of its order ri. Thus each of these powers\nL825@P15: of 2 has at most a 50% probability of agreeing with the previous ones, so all k of them\nL826@P15: agree with probability at most 1/2\nL827@P15: k−1\nL828@P15: , and there is at least a 1 − 1/2\nL829@P15: k−1\nL830@P15: chance that\nL831@P15: the x we choose is good. This scheme will thus work as long as n is odd and not a prime\nL832@P15: power; finding factors of prime powers can be done efficiently with classical methods.\nL833@P15: We now describe the algorithm for finding the order of x (mod n) on a quantum\nL834@P15: computer. This algorithm will use two quantum registers which hold integers represented\nL835@P15: in binary. There will also be some amount of workspace. This workspace gets reset to\nL836@P15: 0 after each subroutine of our algorithm, so we will not include it when we write down\nL837@P15: the state of our machine.\nL838@P15: Given x and n, to find the order of x, i.e., the least r such that x\nL839@P15: r ≡ 1 (mod n), we\nL840@P15: do the following. First, we find q, the power of 2 with n\nL841@P15: 2 ≤ q < 2n2\nL842@P15: . We will not include\nL843@P15: n, x, or q when we write down the state of our machine, because we never change these\nL844@P15: values. In a quantum gate array we need not even keep these values in memory, as they\nL845@P15: can be built into the structure of the gate array.\nL846@P15: Next, we put the first register in the uniform superposition of states representing\nL847@P15: numbers a (mod q). This leaves our machine in state\nL848@P15: 1\nL849@P15: q\nL850@P15: 1/2\nL851@P15: Xq−1\nL852@P15: a=0\nL853@P15: |ai |0i. (5.1)\nL854@P15: This step is relatively easy, since all it entails is putting each bit in the first register into\nL855@P15: the superposition √\nL856@P15: 1\nL857@P15: 2\nL858@P15: (|0i + |1i).\nL859@P15: Next, we compute x\nL860@P15: a\nL861@P15: (mod n) in the second register as described in §3. Since we\nL862@P15: keep a in the first register this can be done reversibly. This leaves our machine in the\nL863@P15: state\nL864@P15: 1\nL865@P15: q\nL866@P15: 1/2\nL867@P15: Xq−1\nL868@P15: a=0\nL869@P15: |ai |x\nL870@P15: a\nL871@P15: (mod n)i. (5.2)\nL872@P15: We then perform our Fourier transform Aq on the first register, as described in §4,\nL873@P15: mapping |ai to\nL874@P15: 1\nL875@P15: q\nL876@P15: 1/2\nL877@P15: Xq−1\nL878@P15: c=0\nL879@P15-16: exp(2πiac/q)|ci. (5.3)FACTORING WITH A QUANTUM COMPUTER 17\nL880@P16: That is, we apply the unitary matrix with the (a, c) entry equal to 1\nL881@P16: q\nL882@P16: 1/2 exp(2πiac/q).\nL883@P16: This leaves our machine in state\nL884@P16: 1\nL885@P16: q\nL886@P16: Xq−1\nL887@P16: a=0\nL888@P16: Xq−1\nL889@P16: c=0\nL890@P16: exp(2πiac/q)|ci |x\nL891@P16: a\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn exponent one-fifth algorithm for deterministic integer factorisation (https://arxiv.org/html/2010.05450)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view0\",\"lineno\":290}); Total lines: 569\nL255: ###### Remark 3.4.\nL256: \nL257: Lemma cite50†3.3 shows that the total number of candidates for $aq+bp$, i.e., the number of integers $x$ such that\nL258: \nL259:  | $$0\\leqslant x-(4kN)^{1/2}<\\frac{N^{1/2}}{4rk^{1/2}}$$  |\nL260: \nL261: for some $k$ in the range $1\\leqslant k\\leqslant r$, is at most\nL262: \nL263:  | $$\\sum_{k=1}^{r}\\left\\lceil\\frac{N^{1/2}}{4rk^{1/2}}\\right\\rceil\\leqslant\\sum_{k=1}^{r}\\left(\\frac{N^{1/2}}{4rk^{1/2}}+1\\right)=O\\left(\\frac{N^{1/2}}{r^{1/2}}+r\\right).$$  |\nL264: Lehman optimises this last expression by taking $r\\asymp N^{1/3}=N^{0.333\\ldots}$, so that the number of candidates is $O(N^{1/3})$. He checks each one individually, using the procedure mentioned in Remark cite51†3.2 , leading to his overall $N^{1/3+o(1)}$ bound.\nL265: Hittmeir [cite35†Hit20 ] takes a smaller value of $r$, namely $r=N^{2/9+o(1)}=N^{0.222\\ldots+o(1)}$, so that the number of candidates increases to $N^{7/18+o(1)}=N^{0.388\\ldots+o(1)}$. (Note that in [cite35†Hit20 ], the parameter $r$ is called $\\eta$.) Taking a smaller value of $r$ introduces more structure into the space of candidates; Hittmeir takes advantage of this by using BSGS techniques to speed up the search instead of examining each candidate individually.\nL266: First, for each $k$ up to $N^{1/9+o(1)}=N^{0.111\\ldots+o(1)}$ (Hittmeir calls this threshold $\\xi$), he runs a BSGS search to examine all candidates corresponding to that one value of $k$. Then he performs one more BSGS search to examine all remaining values of $k$ up to $r=N^{2/9+o(1)}=N^{0.222\\ldots+o(1)}$. Altogether, this leads to an overall complexity of $N^{2/9+o(1)}$.\nL267: Our strategy (see Section cite13†4 ) is to take $r$ smaller again, namely $r=N^{1/5+o(1)}=N^{0.2+o(1)}$. The number of candidates increases even further to $N^{2/5+o(1)}=N^{0.4+o(1)}$. However, we perform a single BSGS search (similar to the method Hittmeir uses for the large values of $k$) that covers all values of $k$ simultaneously. In effect, we enjoy a “square-root speedup” over the entire search space.\nL268: An interesting question is whether it is possible to obtain a fully square-root speedup for Lehman’s original choice $r\\asymp N^{1/3}$. This would presumably lead to a factoring algorithm with complexity $N^{1/6+o(1)}$.\nL269: ## 4. The main algorithm\nL270: Before discussing the main search algorithm, we first consider Algorithm cite39†4.1 , which finds collisions modulo $p$ or $q$ between two subsets of $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$. This subroutine is almost identical to Algorithm 3.2 of [cite35†Hit20 ]. However, whereas Hittmeir allows both subsets to be essentially arbitrary, we specialise to the case that one of the subsets consists entirely of powers of some fixed $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}^{*}_{N}$.\nL271: This allows us to improve the complexity from $O((n+m)\\lg^{3}N)$ (see [cite35†Hit20 , Lem. 3.3]) to $O(n\\lg^{3}N+m\\lg^{2}N)$.\nL272: Algorithm 4.1 – finding collisions modulo $p$ or $q$\nL273: \nL274: Input:\nL275: \nL276:   * –\nL277: \nL278: A positive integer $N$, either prime or semiprime.\nL279: \nL280:   * –\nL281: \nL282: A positive integer $m$, and an element $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ with $\\ord_{N}(\\alpha)\\geqslant m$.\nL283: \nL284:   * –\nL285: \nL286: Elements $v_{1},\\ldots,v_{n}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ for some positive integer $n$, such that $v_{h}\\neq\\alpha^{i}$ for all $h\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ and $i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m-1\\}$.\nL287: \nL288: Output:\nL289: \nL290:   * –\nL291: If $N=pq$ is a semiprime, and if there exists $h\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ such that $v_{h}\\equiv\\alpha^{i}\\pmod{p}$ or $v_{h}\\equiv\\alpha^{i}\\pmod{q}$ for some $i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m-1\\}$, returns $p$ and $q$.\nL292: \nL293:   * –\nL294: \nL295: Otherwise returns “no factors found”.\nL296: \nL297: \nL298: \nL299:   1. 1)\nL300: \nL301: Using Lemma cite47†2.3 (product tree), compute the polynomial\nL302: \nL303:  | $$f(x)\\coloneqq(x-v_{1})\\cdots(x-v_{n})\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}[x].$$  |\nL304: \nL305:   2. 2)\nL306: Using Lemma cite52†2.4 (Bluestein’s algorithm), compute the values $f(\\alpha^{i})\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ for $i=0,\\ldots,m-1$.\nL307: \nL308:   3. 3)\nL309: \nL310: For $i=0,\\ldots,m-1$:\nL311: \nL312:     1. a)\nL313: \nL314: Compute $\\gamma_{i}\\coloneqq\\gcd(N,f(\\alpha^{i}))$.\nL315: \nL316:     2. b)\nL317: \nL318: If $\\gamma_{i}\\notin\\{1,N\\}$, recover $p$ and $q$ and return.\nL319: \nL320:     3. c)\nL321: \nL322: If $\\gamma_{i}=N$, then for $h=1,\\ldots,n$:\nL323: \nL324:       1. i)\nL325: \nL326: If $\\gcd(N,v_{h}-\\alpha^{i})\\neq 1$, recover $p$ and $q$ and return.\nL327: \nL328:   4. 4)\nL329: \nL330: Return “no factors found”.\nL331: ###### Proposition 4.1.\nL332: \nL333: Algorithm cite39†4.1 is correct. For $m,n=O(N)$, its running time is\nL334: \nL335:  | $$O(n\\lg^{3}N+m\\lg^{2}N).$$  |\nL336: ###### Proof.\nL337: We first prove correctness. Suppose that $N=pq$ is a semiprime, and that $v_{h_{0}}\\equiv\\alpha^{i_{0}}$ modulo $p$ or modulo $q$ for some $h_{0}$ and $i_{0}$. Then $f(\\alpha^{i_{0}})$ includes the factor $v_{h_{0}}-\\alpha^{i_{0}}$, so we have $\\gamma_{i_{0}}\\neq 1$ in Step (3a). If additionally $\\gamma_{i_{0}}\\neq N$, then $p$ and $q$ are successfully recovered in Step (3b). Suppose however that $\\gamma_{i_{0}}=N$.\nL338: Then the factors will be recovered in Step (3ci) when we reach $h=h_{0}$, as the preconditions ensure that $\\gcd(N,v_{h_{0}}-\\alpha^{i_{0}})\\neq N$.\nL339: Now we analyse the complexity. By Lemma cite47†2.3 and Lemma cite52†2.4 , Steps (1)–(2) run in time $O(n\\lg^{3}N+m\\lg^{2}N)$. Step (3a) executes at most $m$ times.\nL340: Step (3ci) executes at most $n$ times, because as soon as we find $\\gamma_{i}=N$ in Step (3c), we know that $(v_{1}-\\alpha^{i})\\cdots(v_{n}-\\alpha^{i})=0$ in $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$, so one of the factors $v_{h}-\\alpha^{i}$ must fail to be invertible modulo $N$ and thus reveal the factors $p$ and $q$ (here again we use the hypothesis that $v_{h}\\neq\\alpha^{i}$ in $\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$). Therefore the total number of GCDs computed is at most $m+n$, contributing $O((m+n)\\lg N\\lg^{2}\\lg N)$ to the complexity. ∎\nL341: We now proceed to the main search algorithm (Algorithm cite53†4.2 ). As mentioned previously, this algorithm leverages the search strategy that Hittmeir uses in [cite35†Hit20 ] for the “large” values of $k$, but we carry it out for the entire range of $k$ simultaneously.\nL342: \nL343: Algorithm 4.2 – the main search\nL344: \nL345: Input:\nL346: \nL347:   * –\nL348: \nL349: Positive integers $N\\geqslant 2$ and $r$, such that either $N$ is prime, or $N=pq$ is a semiprime satisfying (cite54†3.1 ), i.e., such that $(N/r)^{1/2}\\leqslant p<N^{1/2}$.\nL350: \nL351:   * –\nL352: A positive integer $m$, and an element $\\alpha\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}^{*}$ with $\\ord_{N}(\\alpha)\\geqslant m$.\nL353: \nL354: Output: If $N$ is a semiprime, returns $p$ and $q$. Otherwise returns “$N$ is prime”.\nL355: \nL356: \nL357: \nL358:   1. 1)\nL359: \nL360: Compute $\\alpha^{i}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}$ for $i=0,\\ldots,m-1$. For each $i=1,\\ldots,m-1$, check whether $\\gcd(N,\\alpha^{i}-1)=1$. If not, return $p$ and $q$.\nL361: \nL362:   2. 2)\nL363: \nL364: For all pairs of positive integers $(a,b)$ such that $1\\leqslant ab\\leqslant r$:\nL365: \nL366:     1. a)\nL367: \nL368: Compute\nL369:  | $$t_{a,b}\\coloneqq\\alpha^{aN+b-\\lceil(4abN)^{1/2}\\rceil}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}.$$  |  | (4.1)\nL370: \nL371:     2. b)\nL372: \nL373: For each integer $j$ in the interval\nL374: \nL375:  | $$0\\leqslant j<\\frac{N^{1/2}}{4rm(ab)^{1/2}},$$  |\nL376: \nL377: compute\nL378: \nL379:  | $$v_{a,b,j}\\coloneqq\\alpha^{-jm}t_{a,b}\\in\\mathbb{Z}_{N}.$$  |  | (4.2)\nL380: \nL381:   3. 3)\nL382: \nL383: Applying a sort-and-match algorithm to the output of Steps (1) and (2), find all $i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m-1\\}$ and all triples $(a,b,j)$ (as in Step (2)) such that\nL384: \nL385:  | $$v_{a,b,j}=\\alpha^{i}.$$  |  | (4.3)\nL386: For each such match, apply Lemma cite55†3.1 to the candidate\nL387: \nL388:  | $$u\\coloneqq i+jm+\\lceil(4abN)^{1/2}\\rceil.$$  |\nL389: \nL390: If Lemma cite55†3.1 succeeds in finding $p$ and $q$, return.\nL391: \nL392:   4. 4)\nL393: Let $v_{1},\\ldots,v_{n}$ be the list of values $v_{a,b,j}$ computed in Step (2), skipping those that were discovered in Step (3) to be equal to $\\alpha^{i}$ for some $i\\in\\{0,\\ldots,m-1\\}$. Apply Algorithm cite39†4.1 to $v_{1},\\ldots,v_{n}$ with the given $N$, $\\alpha$ and $m$. If Algorithm cite39†4.1 succeeds in finding $p$ and $q$, return.\nL394: \nL395:   5. 5)\nL396: \nL397: Return “$N$ is prime”.\nL398: ###### Proposition 4.2.\nL399: \nL400: Algorithm cite53†4.2 is correct. For $r,m=O(N)$, its running time is\nL401: \nL402:  | $$O\\left(\\left(\\frac{N^{1/2}}{r^{1/2}m}+r\\right)\\lg^{4}N+m\\lg^{2}N\\right).$$  |\nL403: ###### Proof.\nL404: \nL405: First suppose that $N$ is a semiprime $pq$ satisfying (cite54†3.1 ). We must prove that in Steps (1)–(4), the algorithm succeeds in finding $p$ and $q$.\nL406: \nL407: If $\\ord_{p}(\\alpha)<m$, then in Step (1) we will discover $p$ and $q$ when we compute $\\gcd(N,\\alpha^{i}-1)$ for $i=\\ord_{p}(\\alpha)$, because the hypothesis $\\ord_{N}(\\alpha)\\geqslant m$ ensures that $\\alpha^{i}\\neq 1$ for this $i$. Henceforth we may assume that $\\ord_{p}(\\alpha)\\geqslant m$.\nL408: Applying Lemma cite50†3.3 , we find that there exist positive integers $a_{0}$ and $b_{0}$, with $1\\leqslant a_{0}b_{0}\\leqslant r$, such that\nL409: \nL410:  | $$0\\leqslant y_{0}<\\frac{N^{1/2}}{4r(a_{0}b_{0})^{1/2}},$$  |\nL411: \nL412: where\nL413: \nL414:  | $$y_{0}=u_{0}-\\lceil(4a_{0}b_{0}N)^{1/2}\\rceil,\\qquad u_{0}=a_{0}q+b_{0}p.$$  |\nL415: \nL416: Observe that\nL417: \nL418:  | $$\\alpha^{u_{0}}=\\alpha^{a_{0}q+b_{0}p}\\equiv\\alpha^{a_{0}pq+b_{0}}\\pmod{p},$$  |\nL419: \nL420: since $p\\equiv 1\\pmod{p-1}$ and $\\alpha^{p-1}\\equiv 1\\pmod{p}$. Thus\nL421:  | $$\\alpha^{y_{0}}\\equiv\\alpha^{a_{0}N+b_{0}-\\lceil(4a_{0}b_{0}N)^{1/2}\\rceil}\\equiv t_{a_{0},b_{0}}\\pmod{p},$$  |\nL422: \nL423: where $t_{a,b}$ is defined as in (cite56†4.1 ). If we now decompose $y_{0}$ as\nL424: \nL425:  | $$y_{0}=i_{0}+j_{0}m,\\qquad 0\\leqslant i_{0}<m,\\qquad 0\\leqslant j_{0}<\\frac{N^{1/2}}{4rm(a_{0}b_{0})^{1/2}},$$  |\nL426: \n"
